## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a robust, scalable prompt orchestration in a multilingual, latency-aware setting; integrates prompts, safety, fallback behavior, and observability.

## Key Concepts
- Intent classification for routing
- Policy-driven module selection
- Latency budgets and data sensitivity
- Cascading failures and fallback strategies
- Observability and auditability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype
function routePrompt(prompt, budget) {
  const intent = classify(prompt);
  const module = policy.choose(intent, prompt.sensitivity, budget.latency);
  const payload = templates[module](prompt);
  return { module, payload };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test ambiguous intents?
- How would you handle dynamic policy updates at runtime?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design multi-tenant prompt systems, policy-driven routing, and drift auditing; checks comfort with latency budgeting and audit trails; reveals understanding of safety constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant policy blobs
- Model routing via data_classifications
- Safety guard injection preserving intent
- Drift detection and latency budgeting
- Audit log design and replayability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype illustrating routing and rewriting
function routePrompt(prompt, tenantPolicy, models) {
  // decide avatar
  const avatar = tenantPolicy.data_classifications.includes('medical') ? models.avatarA : models.avatarB;
  // rewrite to inject guard
  const guarded = `Guarded: ${prompt} [guard:${tenantPolicy.allowed_actions.join(',')}]`;
  // fake latency
  return { guarded, avatar: avatar.name };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale the audit log and ensure tamper-evidence across distributed systems?
- How would you test reliability against prompt paraphrasing or adversarial prompts?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design scalable, policy-aware prompt orchestration with strong privacy guards and precise latency constraints, reflecting real-world multi-tenant systems at scale.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant policy binding
- Guardrails to prevent system prompt leakage
- Modular routing to multiple tools
- Determinism testing under latency budgets
- Structured audit for compliance and debugging

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal routing prototype (conceptual)
function routePrompt(prompt, tenant, policyStore){
  const policy = policyStore[tenant];
  const guarded = applyGuard(prompt, policy);
  return { plan: ["SQL-builder","DataMasker","SummaryFormatter"], guarded };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate non-deterministic outputs across model variants under tight latency? 
- How would you scale tenant policies and audits across many tenants?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design governance with provenance, versioning, and rollback in multi-tenant systems. Focuses on determinism, auditability, and latency budgets under policy drift.

## Key Concepts

- Prompt provenance
- Per-tenant policy versioning
- Immutable audit logs
- Conflict resolution in guard chains
- Latency budgeting

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal TypeScript-like guard pipeline
interface Guard {
  (input: string, ctx: any): string;
}
function redactPII(input: string): string {
  return input.replace(/[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\.[A-Za-z]{2,}/g, '[REDACTED]');
}
function leakageCheck(input: string): string {
  return input.includes('export') ? '[BLOCKED]' : input;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you model policy_version diffs and safely rollback without affecting in-flight prompts?
- How do you ensure audit log integrity in a distributed system?

## Why This Is Asked
Assess ability to optimize prompts for cost and speed, exercising token budgeting and simple NLP heuristics in prompt engineering.

## Key Concepts
- Token counting and budgets
- Intent preservation
- Heuristic trimming

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal compress
function compress(p){
  // naive: drop time qualifiers and rephrase
  let q = p
    .replace(/in the last \d+ months?/i, "")
    .replace(/and export\s+their\s+emails\s+to\s+a\s+CSV\s+file/gi, "export emails to CSV");
  return q.trim();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you evaluate compression ratio vs fidelity?
- How would you handle languages other than English?
